{{nihongo|'''Shigetsuma Honda'''|本多 重間|Honda Shigetsuma|born May 15, 1967}} is a [[Okaiken|Sanese]] politician who has served as the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] since August 25, 2021. A member of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), he has served as its leader non-consecutively since 2009 and has been a member of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] for the Noto 1st District since 1993.

==Early life and education==
Shigetsuma Honda was born on May 15, 1967, in [[Awa, Noto|Awa]], [[Noto Prefecture]], [[Okaiken]]. He was born into the Honda clan, arguably Okaiken's most prominent political-historical dynasty. His father, [[Tadashige Honda]], served as Prime Minister from 1961 to 1964 and was known as the "Mitoyagi Shogun" for his iron-fisted leadership style. His uncle, [[Shigehara Honda]], also served as Prime Minister from 1990 to 1993.

The Honda family holds a significant historical legacy, having served as the hereditary shoguns of the [[Mitoyagi Empire]] from 1605 until the [[Saito Restoration]] in 1871. This long-standing political lineage can be traced back to the Sengoku period, where figures like the general [[Honda Shigetada]] played a crucial role under [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] during the unification of the island of [[Haru]]. The clan's political influence reached its zenith with [[Honda Shigetomo]], who became the second shogun of the Mitoyagi Shogunate. Growing up, Shigetsuma was frequently reminded by his father that their family were "rulers by blood, not just by ballot," a sentiment that instilled in him a profound sense of noblesse oblige and historical destiny.

===Education and Father's Influence===
From a young age, Shigetsuma was groomed by his father to eventually lead the nation. Tadashige, who had retired from the premiership but remained a powerful factional leader (the "Shadow Shogun"), took an intense personal interest in his son's education. While Shigetsuma initially showed interest in history and literature, Tadashige strictly forbade these pursuits, dismissing them as "hobbies for idle aristocrats." Instead, he pushed his son toward the study of statecraft and economics, viewing them as the only practical tools for governance.

Yielding to his father's pressure, Shigetsuma enrolled at the prestigious [[Okami University]] in 1986, majoring in Political Science. During his university years, he lived a disciplined life under the watchful eye of his father's secretaries, avoiding the student radicalism that characterized the late 1980s. His thesis, titled "The Necessity of Decisive Executive Power in Parliamentary Systems," strongly reflected his father's philosophy that Okaiken required strong, centralized leadership to thrive.

After graduating from Okami University in 1990, Tadashige insisted that his son gain an international perspective on economics, believing that the next generation of Sanese leaders would need to navigate a globalized market. Shigetsuma traveled to the United States to attend the [[Wharton School of the University of Acadia]], where he earned an MBA. This period abroad was transformative; while he retained his father's belief in strong leadership, exposure to Western free-market principles softened some of the statist instincts he had inherited. Upon returning to Okaiken in 1993, he briefly worked at a major trading house before becoming his father's executive secretary, marking his formal entry into the world of politics.

==Political career==
[[File:Honda_Being_interviewed_on_TV.png|thumb|left|250px|Honda Shigetsuma being interviewed by the press following the 2021 election victory.]]
Honda's political career began in 1993 when he was elected to the House of Commoners for the Noto 1st District, succeeding a retiring incumbent who had served as a loyal retainer to the Honda family for decades. His entry into politics was viewed by many as the inevitable continuation of the Honda dynasty, yet Shigetsuma immediately sought to differentiate himself from the "Shogun style" of his father.

===Representative===
Following his election to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in the [[1993 Okaiken general election|1993 general election]], Honda was appointed to the Committee on Foreign Affairs. Leveraging his fluency in Trent and his education at Wharton, he quickly established a reputation as a policy hawk and an internationalist, often diverging from the traditional "pork-barrel" politics that characterized the rural wing of his party. His maiden speech in the Diet, which argued for the necessity of Sanese participation in international peacekeeping operations, was controversial but marked him as a rising star willing to challenge pacifist orthodoxy.

He was re-elected with increased margins in the [[1996 Okaiken general election|1996]] and [[2000 Okaiken general election|2000]] general elections. During this period, he focused heavily on the economic revitalization of the Noto Peninsula, which was suffering from depopulation. He was instrumental in passing the "Regional Heritage Tourism Act" of 2002, which provided federal subsidies to historical castle towns like [[Awa]] to develop tourism infrastructure. This policy is widely credited with transforming Awa into a major domestic tourist destination, modernizing the port district while preserving the historical ''shitamachi'' areas. The success of this initiative demonstrated his ability to deliver tangible results to his constituents without relying on the heavy industrial subsidies favored by the LDP.

In 2003, Honda was appointed as the Chairman of the PPO's Committee on Administrative Reform. In this role, he aggressively pursued the privatization of the state-owned postal service, a move that alienated older party stalwarts who relied on the postal vote but endeared him to younger, urban voters and the business community. His ability to bridge the gap between the PPO's rural base and urban reformists earned him the moniker "The Bridge of Noto."

He served as the Shadow Senior Vice Minister of Finance from 2005 to 2007, where he advocated for fiscal discipline and a reduction in the national debt. His tenure was marked by a clash with the powerful agricultural lobby, as he sought to reduce inefficiencies in farm subsidies. While this cemented his image as a "maverick" within the establishment, it also created friction with the party elders who viewed his technocratic approach as a threat to the PPO's traditional support structures.

===First leadership term and 2013 "Crash"===
Honda's rise through the ranks of the PPO accelerated in the late 2000s. In 2009, following the party's defeat in the general election, he was elected party leader, succeeding Junpei Nomura. At just 42 years old, he represented a generational shift for the Progressive Party. He led the party into the 2009 general election, which resulted in a minor loss of 18 seats. Despite the loss, he retained the confidence of the party and remained as Leader of the Opposition, tasked with rebuilding the PPO's platform against the ruling Liberal Democratic Party.

However, his first leadership term ended in disaster. The political landscape shifted dramatically with the rise of [[Toshiro Kurosawa]] and his "Jutsu Restoration" movement within the LDP. Honda, adhering to traditional fiscal conservatism and moderate liberalism, struggled to counter Kurosawa's populist energy and aggressive nationalist rhetoric. In the snap election of February 2013, the PPO was decimated. The party crashed from 120 seats to just 35, the worst result in its history since 1966. The defeat was a humiliation for the Honda clan; critics argued that Shigetsuma lacked the "iron fist" of his father and the tactical acumen of his uncle. Facing overwhelming pressure and a mutiny from the surviving MPs, Honda accepted full responsibility for the "catastrophic" defeat and resigned as party leader. He was replaced by the interim leader Okuma Shigemasa.

===Political comeback===
Honda spent the next year in the political wilderness, effectively exiled to the backbenches. During this period, he reportedly considered leaving politics entirely but was persuaded by his uncle, former Prime Minister [[Shigehara Honda]], to remain and fight. He spent 2014 traveling the country, holding town halls, and publishing a treatise titled "The Path to Sanese Renewal", which outlined a new vision for the PPO combining social safety nets with market incentives.

On December 14, 2014, he staged a dramatic political comeback. Utilizing the support he had cultivated among younger MPs and the rank-and-file membership, he successfully challenged the incumbent leader Okuma Shigemasa for the party leadership. He won the leadership contest by a narrow margin, running on a platform of "Attonement and Rebirth," promising to rebuild the party from the ground up.

===Deputy Prime Minister (2016–2021)===
He led the resurgent PPO into the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 general election]]. Capitalizing on the implosion of the Kurosawa and Tanizaki LDP administrations, the PPO saw a massive rebound, winning 131 seats. However, the true winner of the election was the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), which had capitalized on anti-establishment sentiment. As leader of the second-largest party in the opposition bloc, Honda made the pragmatic—and controversial—decision to enter a "left-wing" coalition government with the SCP under Prime Minister [[Sho Aoyagi]].

Honda was appointed as Okaiken's first-ever Deputy Prime Minister on July 25, 2016, also taking the portfolio of Minister of State for Economic Policy. The coalition was an "unholy alliance" between the Marxist Aoyagi and the Wharton-educated aristocrat Honda. During the five-year coalition, Honda often served as the "moderate face" of the government, tasked with calming international markets and maintaining relations with [[Shayden]] while the SCP pursued radical domestic policies. He frequently clashed with Aoyagi over the budget, successfully blocking the SCP's most extreme nationalization plans while allowing their social welfare expansions to pass. This period restored Honda's reputation as a competent statesman who could ensure stability even in chaotic circumstances.

===2021 General Election===
By 2021, the SCP government was suffering from high inflation and voter fatigue. Sensing the shifting tide and seeing an opportunity to reclaim the mantle of leadership, Honda formally withdrew the PPO from the coalition in August 2021, effectively collapsing the Aoyagi government and triggering a general election.

In the subsequent campaign, Honda ran on a platform of "Stable Progress," positioning the PPO as the "adult in the room"—a responsible alternative to both the reckless spending of the Communists and the stagnation of the far-right LDP. His debate performances were widely praised; he effectively dismantled Aoyagi's economic record while portraying the LDP's [[Hakuya Terakubo]] as a dangerous reactionary. The strategy paid off; in the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]], the PPO won 201 seats, becoming the largest party in the Diet and securing a mandate for Honda to form the next government.

==Prime Minister of Okaiken==
Honda was sworn in as Prime Minister on August 25, 2021, leading the third PPO-led government in Okaiken's history and the first since his uncle's term ended in 1993.

===First term (2021–2025)===
[[File:Shigetsuma Speech.png|thumb|left|250px|Honda Shigetsuma delivering a policy speech to the Diet in favor of the National Universal Health Act, 2023.]]

Following the defeat of the Aoyagi administration in the 2021 general election, the Progressive Party of Okaiken (PPO) emerged as the largest party but fell short of the 238 seats required for an absolute majority in the House of Commoners. Rather than forming a formal coalition, Honda opted to form a minority government, relying on a confidence and supply agreement with the centrist [[New Komeito Party]] (NKP) and the leftist [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party]] (SDP). This fragile arrangement required Honda to negotiate legislation on a case-by-case basis, a test of the consensus-building skills he had learned from his uncle, [[Shigehara Honda]].

====The National Universal Health Act====
The defining achievement of Honda's first term was the passage of the [[National Universal Health Act]] in 2023. For decades, Okaiken's healthcare system had been a patchwork of employer-based insurance and fragmented municipal plans. Honda proposed a radical overhaul: the creation of a single-payer "Sanese National Health Service" (SNHS) guaranteeing free coverage at the point of service for all citizens.

The legislative battle was fierce. The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), led by the fiscal hawk [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], vehemently opposed the bill. Sadanuma argued that the projected tax increases required to fund the system would "bankrupt the nation" and strangle the private sector. The debates in the Budget Committee were some of the most contentious in postwar history, with LDP members walking out of the chamber in protest during the final vote.

Despite the intense opposition, Honda successfully whipped the votes of the SDP and NKP, arguing that universal health coverage was a moral imperative for a modern nation. The Act passed the House of Officers in June 2023. While the policy was broadly popular with the public and contributed to a temporary boost in Honda's approval ratings, the subsequent consumption tax hike from 8% to 12% to fund the program provided the opposition with powerful ammunition for the next election cycle.

====Immigration Reform and Labor Shortages====
Faced with a rapidly aging population and a shrinking workforce, the Honda administration enacted a series of liberal immigration reforms in 2022 and 2024. The "New Horizons Visa Program" significantly lowered the barriers for skilled and semi-skilled workers from Southeast Fosia to enter Okaiken, particularly in the nursing care, construction, and agricultural sectors.

This policy proved to be a political minefield. The conservative wing of the LDP criticized the reforms as a threat to social cohesion and national security, with Shadow Foreign Minister [[Reo Nakasone]] warning of "irreversible cultural dilution." Conversely, the government's confidence partner, the SDP, also opposed aspects of the reforms, arguing that an influx of foreign labor would suppress wages for the Sanese working class. Honda was forced to navigate a narrow path between these criticisms, ultimately passing a watered-down version of the bill that included strict caps on annual intakes and mandatory language proficiency tests.

====Economic Performance====
Economically, the first Honda cabinet presided over a period of steady if unspectacular growth. The administration continued the digitalization initiatives begun under previous governments and invested heavily in green energy infrastructure. However, by late 2024, the "En Crisis"—a sharp depreciation of the Sanese currency—began to erode real wages. The rising cost of living, exacerbated by the tax hikes for the Health Act, led to a decline in the government's popularity heading into the 2025 general election.

===2025 general election===
Honda's PPO entered the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]] campaign with a comfortable lead, running on a platform of "Keep the Growth Going." However, the campaign was seen as uninspired by media commentators. During the first leaders' debate, Honda committed a major gaffe when he dismissed concerns over the inflation caused by his healthcare act, stating, "It is a cost we can afford." This remark was widely attacked by opponents as tone-deaf.

On election night, the PPO suffered a disappointing loss of 20 seats, finishing with 181 and again failing to secure a majority. The LDP saw a major resurgence, while the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP)]], under its new leader [[Rento Nakayama]], had a historic comeback, winning 50 seats and becoming the "kingmaker" party.

===Second term (2025–present)===
The general election of October 27, 2025, dealt a significant blow to the PPO, which lost 20 seats and its dominance in the lower house. However, as the leader of the largest party (181 seats), Honda retained the mandate to form a government. Facing a resurgent LDP under [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], Honda acted quickly to prevent an opposition blockade. He entered into intense negotiations with the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), the election's breakout success story.

On November 4, 2025, Honda an d OSP leader [[Rento Nakayama]] announced the formation of a formal coalition government. The agreement combined the PPO's 181 seats with the OSP's 50, resulting in a bloc of 231 seats—seven short of an absolute majority. This necessitated a fragile governance style, requiring the coalition to seek additional support from minor parties like the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party]] (SDP) or the [[Okaiken Innovation Party]] (OIP) on a vote-by-vote basis.

Honda was formally re-appointed as Prime Minister by the Emperor on November 7, 2025. In a move widely seen as a strategic concession to the populist wave, he appointed the 32-year-old Nakayama as Minister of Health and Welfare. This appointment placed the OSP leader in charge of implementing and potentially expanding the [[National Universal Health Act]], a policy area central to the Socialist platform.

The new administration, dubbed the "Honda-Nakayama Cabinet," faces immediate challenges. The opposition LDP has aggressively attacked the coalition's legitimacy, with Sadanuma labeling it an "unstable marriage of convenience" that threatens fiscal discipline. Early parliamentary sessions have been dominated by debates over the budget, with Honda attempting to balance his party's growth-oriented "New Capitalism" with the OSP's demands for increased subsidies for child care and mental health services. Despite the friction, Honda has framed the coalition as a necessary evolution, stating in his inaugural address, "We have heard the voice of the people; they demand stability, but they also demand care. We shall provide both."

====The "Winter of Discontent" and Budget Deadlock (2026)====

[[File:Nakayama Meeting with Shigetsuma Honda.png|thumb|left|300px|Honda (right) and Nakayama (left) during a high-stakes Budget Committee hearing in February 2026.]]
As of February 2026, the coalition is facing its first existential crisis, dubbed by the media as the "Winter of Discontent". The "En Crisis"—a sharp 20% depreciation of the Sanese currency against major global benchmarks—accelerated in late January, driving up the cost of imported energy and staples during a particularly harsh winter. This loss of purchasing power has sparked localized protests in districts like [[Tatara]], where the price of heating oil and basic groceries has nearly doubled since the 2025 election.

The crisis has exposed deep ideological rifts within the cabinet. Minister of Health [[Rento Nakayama]] has publicly demanded an emergency "Inflation Relief Package" worth 5 trillion [[Sanese En]], including direct cash handouts to low-income households and a freeze on scheduled health insurance premium hikes. Nakayama threatened that the Socialist Party would boycott the FY2026 budget vote if these demands were not met, stating, "We did not join this government to watch the poor freeze".

In contrast, Prime Minister Honda and his Finance Ministry team have argued that further deficit spending would trigger a collapse of the bond market and weaken the En even further. Honda has proposed a more modest package focused on targeted energy subsidies, seeking to maintain Okaiken's credit rating.

====Committee Hearings and the 15% Solution====
On February 15, 2026, Honda was summoned to the Diet's Budget Committee for a grueling twelve-hour testimony. The opposition [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] has seized on the paralysis. Leader of the Opposition [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] filed a motion of no confidence within the committee, arguing that the government is "functionally brain-dead" and unable to pass a budget before the April 1st deadline. Sadanuma pointed to the 15% hike in imported grain prices as evidence that Honda's "New Capitalism" was failing to protect the domestic market.

Honda's strategy during the hearings has been one of "Calculated Concession." In a surprising maneuver on February 20, he offered a "15% Energy Rebate" funded not by new debt, but by a temporary "Windfall Levy" on the very [[Global 500]] companies headquartered in [[Okami]] that he had previously protected. This move was designed to satisfy Nakayama’s populist base while appearing fiscally responsible to the international markets.

====Backroom Negotiations with the OIP====
With the government lacking an absolute majority (sitting at 231 of 238 required seats), Honda has engaged in frantic backroom negotiations with the minor [[Okaiken Innovation Party]] (OIP) to survive the impending confidence vote. Led by [[Kei Yamada]], the OIP has demanded a total deregulation of the [[Okaiken Railways Group]]'s non-rail land assets in exchange for their seven votes. 

This demand has placed Honda in a "Triple Bind":
* '''The PPO Base:''' Fears that deregulating railway land will destroy the character of historic districts like [[Tatarabashi]].
* '''The OSP Partners:''' Nakayama views land deregulation as a "gift to the developers" he has spent his career fighting.
* '''Survival:''' Without the OIP, the budget fails, the government falls, and Okaiken faces a snap election in the middle of a currency collapse.

As of late February, a tentative "Midnight Agreement" is rumored to have been reached, involving a "Dual-Track Budget" that provides Nakayama’s subsidies in the form of digital En vouchers—a concession to Digital Minister [[Nao Matsuzaka]]—while granting the OIP limited "Innovation Zones" around Aoi (Gakushuin-mae) Station.

====The Nakayama Tragedy and the "March Maelstrom" (2026)====
On March 1, 2026, the fragile stability of the Honda-Nakayama Cabinet was shattered by the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]], where a 17-year-old student was killed by a one-man operated train on the OR Okayama Line. The incident triggered a period of nationwide civil unrest and industrial action known as the March Maelstrom.

Honda’s initial response was characterized by political analysts as a case study in technocratic detachment. During an emergency Diet session on March 4, Honda defended the one-man operation mandate as economically vital for the modernization of Okaiken and a cost-efficient necessity in the face of the ongoing En Crisis. His statement that the country must accept certain operational adjustments to maintain fiscal discipline was widely interpreted by the public as prioritizing corporate efficiency over human life, leading to the popular opposition slogan Modernization over Mortality.

The crisis intensified on March 6 after leaked video evidence from the Nakayama incident appeared to show the train driver distracted by a company-issued iPhone, which was being used to monitor real-time scheduling as part of Honda's Digital Rail Initiative. This revelation directly linked Honda's push for digital modernization to the fatal safety failure. The resulting Mirror Yoshi protest movement saw hand-drawn mirrors and red seiri-ken tickets affixed to PPO offices across Okaiken, symbolizing the driver’s failure to perform mandatory physical safety checks (shisakanko) in favor of digital monitoring.

====Coalition Collapse and Functional Paralyzation====
The tragedy effectively neutralized the Midnight Agreement with the [[Okaiken Innovation Party]] (OIP), as the party sought to distance itself from the administration's rail policies. On March 9, a high-stakes cabinet meeting resulted in a televised standoff between Honda and Health Minister [[Rento Nakayama]]. Honda’s refusal to issue a nationwide moratorium on one-man operations led to Nakayama publicly clearing his office, signaling the functional end of the coalition.

By March 11, 2026, the Honda government was reduced to a minority status following the formal withdrawal of the Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP). Compounded by a nationwide rail strike led by the [[Okaiken Transit Workers Union]], Honda’s administration was described by media outlets as functionally brain-dead. As of March 11, Honda has retreated to the Kantei to prepare for a formal vote of no confidence scheduled by the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] for March 12.

==Personal life==
Honda is married to Sugimoto Honoka. They were married in 1990 and have four children: Honda Shigenori, Honda Norishige, Honda Shigenao, and Honda Masako.

